{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790958912,"nonce":"H+gvGhzD3MODIOZnpZIblEmVdPE=","title":"A Single returned through a generic interface reads as 0","message":"A method returning `Single`, called through a generic interface instantiated\nat `Single`, returns 0. Called directly, the same method returns the right\nvalue. Reproduces in every mode (interp, hybrid, jit), on the installed build\nof 2026-09-28 and on target/release of 2026-09-30 (HEAD b3e87e4), macOS arm64.\n\n    interface IG\u003cT\u003e { function get():T; }\n\n    class G implements IG\u003cSingle\u003e {\n        public function new() {}\n        public function get():Single return 200;\n    }\n\n    class Repro {\n        static function main() {\n            var direct = new G();\n            var viaIface:IG\u003cSingle\u003e = direct;\n            trace(direct.get());    // 200\n            trace(viaIface.get());  // 0, expected 200\n        }\n    }\n\n    haxe -main Repro -hl repro.hl \u0026\u0026 ash --mode interp repro.hl\n\nWhat is not involved:\n- The native boundary. An HDLL primitive declared `DEFINE_PRIM(_F32, ...)`\n  returns 200 to Haxe in every mode.\n- Other instantiations. The same shape at `Int` and at `Float` returns the\n  right value.\n- Boxing on its own. `var d:Dynamic = (200 : Single); var back:Single = d;`\n  reads back 200.\n\nSo it is the call through the interface slot whose return is typed `T` that\nloses an f32 specifically.\n\nFound through ashui (Haxe bindings to the Blinc UI runtime), whose\n`Signal\u003cSingle\u003e` is an abstract over `ISignal\u003cT\u003e`: every read of an f32\nsignal through it returns 0, including the reads a computed makes, so a\nlayout size derived from an f32 signal comes out 0.","files":null}]}